way i never had before  but honestly i didn't love everything  about it  there are some parts that i found hard  to read because of the social commentary  about  gender and class that just doesn't land  well today  the story is set on a two-dimensional  plane and the narrator  a self-described gentleman is a square  shapes with more sides are higher in  class  hexagons are noble and something with  more sides than that  will approach the priestly order lower  class workmen  such as squares butler are triangles  women are a single straight line  there's his wife right there square's  house  is a pentagon but this top down view is  not how he sees it  to him all things look like lines of  varying size  but he can feel the vertices and  determine the angle  square visits line land which is only  one dimensional  and wishes that he could communicate to  the inhabitants  that there is a second dimension out  there but when square  is visited in flatland by a sphere from  a third dimension  square finds it hard to accept because  he can't imagine a third dimension  square wonders if this is true then what  would come next  and asks sphere if there is a fourth  dimension  sphere says no that's ridiculous  but really they're having the same  problem as square  they simply cannot imagine a dimension  above their own  overall it's a worthwhile mind bender  and it shows that even a book about math  can't be totally separated from the  culture  in which it was written moving on to  calculus  this is what seems like a fairly  standard calculus textbook  the first line of it is any situation  involving a rate of change is described  mathematically  in the language of calculus it starts  with the idea of a gradient  then works through differentiation areas  under a curve  and integration then we've got circular  functions motion  curves and parametric equations  a visit to infinite series and  exponentials and logarithms there's also  differential equations  hyperbolic functions and curves and  surfaces  some of the stuff i met at the end of  high school  or at university and much of it i've  forgotten  i've slept on my integration so long i  can barely do the basics  so this book would do me well as a  refresher  it seems clear enough although from what  i can see  it doesn't always explain too much of  the why  behind some of the definitions it gives  revision questions  after every few chapters with answers  which i like  someone who checked this out in the past  maybe this year or maybe 39 years ago  when it was published  has made a lot of annotations in pencil  i find these comforting  amongst all the seriousness of the math  there's  a human reminder and a source of company  it's a reminder as i try to decipher  each example  that i'm not the only one who's been  here they even left  a little to-do list saturday arvo read  notes  this book would have just been published  when terence read it  which is maybe why he picked it it is my  opinion that when it comes to  introductory calculus textbooks  they all pretty much cover the same sort  of stuff so  if your goal is to learn calculus then  the best book for you  is probably just the one that you have  access to  or it might be worth checking out a few  sample pages from a few options  to see which of the explanations you  prefer  on young terence's reading list there is  also  matrices and vectors numbers  inequalities and linear programming  readings in mathematics  and i found this picture of him reading  continued fractions  by olds at age nine also on the list  is the international mathematics  olympiad a book of past problems  and this is interesting because terence  would go on to become the youngest  competitor in this olympiad  at age 10. he won a bronze silver and  then a gold medal at age 13  making him the youngest winner of each  of these categories  and his two brothers nigel and trevor  have also represented australia  at this competition this next one is the  elements o